{"type":"assistant","message":{"model":"claude-sonnet-5-5","id":"msg_011CgVF5sFtrdFy3ywMrZMco","type":"message","role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"text","text":"**Hypothesis:** A customer's reminder date should be 14 days before their renewal, and the renewal should be one year after their annual plan began. The job can fail at both, and it does for some customers. It would be false if every customer's reminder matched that rule.\n\n**System:** Reproduced for both customers. I ran `reminder_date` on `customers.csv`:\n\n| Customer | Plan start | Code's renewal | Code's reminder | What it should be |\n|---|---|---|---|---|\n| C-311 | 2026-03-10 (annual), 2025-11-02 (signup) | 2026-11-02 | **2026-10-19** | renewal 2027-03-10, reminder 2027-02-24 |\n| C-327 | 2023-06-01 | 2024-05-31 | **2024-05-17** | renewal 2024-06-01, reminder 2024-05-18 |\n| C-302 | 2024-08-15 | 2025-08-15 | 2025-08-01 | correct |\n| C-340 | 2025-01-20 | 2026-01-20 | 2026-01-06 | correct |\n\n**Findings (all from the running code):** There are two separate bugs in `renewal_date` (`reminders.py:8`).\n1. **Wrong anchor (C-311).** It reads `signup`, not `annual_since`. C-311 signed up in November 2025 and moved to annual in March 2026. The code counts a year from the signup, so the reminder fires in October 2026. That is about 4.5 months before their real renewal.\n2. **Fixed 365 days (C-327).** It adds `timedelta(days=365)`, not one calendar year. The span from 2023-06-01 to 2024-06-01 contains 29 February 2024, so it is 366 days. The date lands one day early, on May 31, and the reminder lands on May 17. That is 15 days before the actual renewal. The drift is one day per leap day crossed.\n3. **Why the test missed both.** `test_two_weeks_before` uses C-340. That customer has `signup == annual_since`, and their year has no 29 February, so neither bug shows. C-302 is correct for the same reason. The test passes and can't catch either bug.\n4. **Related, not reported.** The renewal is always the first anniversary of the anchor date. A customer in their second year or later never gets another reminder. For example, C-327's only computed reminder was in 2024. I haven't checked whether the nightly job handles this elsewhere. The job itself isn't in this directory.\n\n**Decide:**\n1. **Renewal anchor.** Should it be `annual_since` plus one year? *Default: yes.* This assumes the term starts when the annual plan begins. The billing system might instead keep the original cycle or prorate. This directory has no billing data, so I can't confirm it.\n2. **Year arithmetic.** *Default: add one calendar year.* A 29 February start would then need a rule. Options are February 28 (my default) or March 1.\n3. **Reminders already sent.** C-311 got a wrong reminder. Should we send a correction? That is customer-facing, so I won't do it unasked.\n\n**Scope:** in: `renewal_date` anchor and year arithmetic. A regression test using C-311 and C-327. out: multi-year renewals (finding 4), unless you want them in.\n\n**Risk:** Customer-facing emails. Wrong dates mean early or late reminders. They don't cause data loss or billing changes. The fix is a small code change that is easy to undo. Customers affected beyond these two are unknown, because I only have a 4-row export. Any customer whose plan began after a 29 February, or who changed plans, may be wrong.\n\n**Claims** (each with an example and what would falsify it):\n- **c1, retire:** renewal is based on `signup`. Example: C-311's reminder becomes 2027-02-24. Falsified if C-311 still gets 2026-10-19.\n- **c2, retire:** the renewal is computed as 365 days. Example: C-327's renewal is 2024-06-01, so the reminder is 2024-05-18. Falsified if C-327 still gets 2024-05-17.\n- **c3, preserve:** customers where it already works don't change. Example: C-302 stays 2025-08-01 and C-340 stays 2026-01-06. Falsified if either changes.\n\n**Not explored:** the real billing renewal dates, the nightly job and its scheduler, the full customer table, timezones, and how many customers were affected. I made no code changes. I have not written the red test yet, to respect \"don't change the code\". It would be the first check in the build.\n\n**Ask:** Vote GO, NO-GO, or HOLD."}],"container":null,"stop_reason":null,"stop_sequence":null,"stop_details":null,"usage":{"input_tokens":2,"cache_creation_input_tokens":729,"cache_read_input_tokens":40029,"cache_creation":{"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":0,"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":729},"output_tokens":7,"service_tier":"standard","inference_geo":"global","speed":"standard"},"input_transformations":[],"diagnostics":null,"context_management":null},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"722710c8-076c-4aa2-b996-b6f629412ae1","uuid":"e050b195-0035-459f-95b7-7f1c0c7f8f42","timestamp":"2026-10-28T20:11:33.205Z","request_id":"req_011CgVF5rxXGe9mA6PKjdBDM"}
